</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion Large Language Models (DLLMs) promise fast non-autoregressive inference but suffer a severe quality-speed trade-off in parallel decoding. This stems from the “combinatorial contradiction” phenomenon, where parallel tokens form semantically inconsistent combinations. We address this by integrating continuous representations into the discrete decoding process, as they preserve rich inter-position dependency.
We propose ReMix (Rejection Mixing), a framework that introduces a novel Continuous Mixing State as an intermediate between the initial masked state and the final decoded token state. This intermediate state allows a token’s representation to be iteratively refined in a continuous space, resolving mutual conflicts with other tokens before collapsing into a final discrete sample. Furthermore, a rejection rule reverts uncertain representations from the continuous state back to the masked state for reprocessing, ensuring stability and preventing error propagation.
ReMix thus mitigates combinatorial contradictions by enabling continuous-space refinement during discrete diffusion decoding. Extensive experiments demonstrate that ReMix, as a training-free method, achieves a 2-8<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> inference speedup without any quality degradation.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
          
          
          
          
        Code: <a href="https://github.com/Serpientw/ReMix-DLLM" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://github.com/Serpientw/ReMix-DLLM</a>.</span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span><sup id="footnotex1.1" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup>Equal contribution. <sup id="footnotex1.2" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>Corresponding author.</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proliferation of Large Language Models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> marks a pivotal moment in artificial intelligence.
Yet their predominant autoregressive (AR) architecture, which generates outputs in a token-by-token sequence, severely limits inference speed.
To address this, Diffusion Large Language Models (DLLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> have emerged as a compelling non-AR paradigm. By modelling entire sequences of tokens in parallel, DLLMs bypass
the inherent dependencies of AR models, offering the potential for a substantial leap in generative throughput and establishing them as a critical frontier for developing high-efficiency language and multimodal systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.22868v1/Intro.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/473;" width="685" height="473" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The ReMix decoding process and its impact on the quality-speed trade-off. (Left) By introducing an intermediate Continuous State (<math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math>), ReMix extends the standard <math id="S1.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}\rightarrow T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}\rightarrow T</annotation></semantics></math> transition by introducing a flexible loop involving Mixing <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐂</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, self-transition <math id="S1.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}\circlearrowright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>↻</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}\circlearrowright</annotation></semantics></math> and Rejection <math id="S1.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}\rightarrow\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}\rightarrow\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling in-process refinement. (Right) On GSM8K, ReMix demonstrates superior performance, turning the baseline’s 7.05% accuracy loss (when accelerating 3.39x) into a 2.80% accuracy gain while achieving similar acceleration.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This parallel promise, however, is undermined by a critical dilemma: a severe quality-speed trade-off.
They achieve optimal performance when decoding a single token at a time, but the quality degrades significantly when attempting to decode multiple tokens in parallel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.
This trade-off is a direct symptom of a fundamental problem we term the “combinatorial contradiction”, where tokens sampled within the same decoding step can be mutually contradictory. The issue is exemplified by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Zhou [38]</a></cite>: “The list of poker hands that consist of two English words are: _ _.” While valid subsequent pairs include “high card”, “two pair”, “full house”, or “straight flush”, a direct parallel approach might independently select the most probable token for each position, resulting in a semantically incorrect combination like “high house”. Although sophisticated parallel decoding schedules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> have improved the trade-off between quality and speed, they do not fully resolve the core consistency problem. WINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> mitigates this issue through a revocable strategy, but at the cost of introducing an additional verification block and its associated computational overhead. APD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, on the other hand, addresses this challenge by defining a multiplicative mixture between the DLLM marginal probabilities and the joint probability of sequences under a small auxiliary AR model, a method that, while effective, requires additional computation and enforces a left-to-right generation order.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In existing DLLMs,
“combinatorial contradiction”
arises from the discrete decoding nature: tokens generated within the same step cannot perceive one another and are sampled independently
 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.
Continuous representations offer a way out of this limitation:
they enable the model to capture dependencies across different positions in the sequence and preserve richer information throughout the decoding process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
To address combinatorial contradiction, we propose a novel approach that introduces continuous representations into the decoding process. By retaining continuous representations of unfilled positions throughout the decoding process, rather than remasking them and discarding valuable information, we preserve the interdependencies between positions in the sequence. This allows the model to iteratively refine the continuous representations over multiple decoding steps, resolving mutual contradictions and ensuring combinatorial consistency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Building on this intuition,
we propose ReMix (Rejection Mixing), a method that integrates continuous states into the discrete diffusion decoding process to address the “combinatorial contradiction” (see <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Rejection Mixing: Fast Semantic Propagation of Mask Tokensfor Efficient DLLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
In the standard decoding process, each token position transitions from a [MASK] state to a semantic token. Traditional methods use predefined rules to control this transition. ReMix introduces an intermediate continuous state, allowing token representations to be refined before finalizing them as discrete tokens. To ensure stability, we also incorporate a rejection mechanism that reverts uncertain representations back to the [MASK] state for reprocessing, preventing early uncertainty from affecting the decoding process.
Our main contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze a fundamental bottleneck in the parallel decoding of DLLMs, which we term the “combinatorial contradiction”. We posit that this issue stems from the purely discrete nature of the decoding process and propose a novel approach to resolve it by integrating continuous representations into the discrete diffusion loop.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose ReMix (Rejection Mixing), a novel, training-free decoding framework. ReMix introduces a Continuous Mixing State as an intermediate step, enabling iterative refinement and ensuring stability through a rejection mechanism. This approach allows for resolving mutual dependencies in a continuous space before discretization, improving the overall decoding process.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive experiments on both language and multimodal generation benchmarks. Our results demonstrate that ReMix successfully breaks the severe quality-speed trade-off, achieving a 2-8<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> inference speedup over strong baselines without any quality degradation, and in several cases, even improving output quality.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Diffusion-based Large Language Model</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, initially gaining prominence for image generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, have recently emerged as a competitive alternative to AR language models in text generation. This transition from continuous to discrete data domains necessitates new modeling approaches. The foundational work by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al. [37]</a></cite> first explored this direction, which was later rigorously formalized by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al. [1]</a></cite> in the D3PM framework. D3PM conceptualizes the diffusion forward process as a discrete-state Markov chain with a series of transition matrices, which is later extended by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al. [5]</a></cite> using the theory of continuous-time Markov chains (CTMCs).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A particularly influential thread of research, known as masked diffusion models (MDMs), builds upon the absorbing-state diffusion concept in D3PM. As MDMs have evolved, they have demonstrated compelling performance across scales, from compact architectures like SEDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, MDLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> and RADD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> to large-scale implementations such as LLaDA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> and Dream <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>. Most recently, this line of work has been expanded to multimodal learning. MMaDA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, for instance, introduces a family of multimodal large diffusion models featuring a shared probabilistic formulation and a modality-agnostic architecture, underscoring the versatility of the diffusion framework.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.22868v1/ReMix_v3.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/429;" width="685" height="429" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">ReMix resolves combinatorial contradictions by allowing continuous-space refinement. Using the poker hand example, this figure compares the decoding processes. (Top) Vanilla Parallel Decoding: Succumbs to the contradiction. It independently decodes “Full” and “Pair” at positions 11 and 12, resulting in the semantically incorrect “Full Pair” in 8 steps. (Middle) ReMix: Correctly generates “Full House” in only 4 steps.
(Bottom) ReMix Output Distribution: This visualizes the internal mechanism. In Steps 1 &amp; 2, both positions are in the Continuous Mixing State (State <math id="S2.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, gradient). Their output distributions are iteratively refined, allowing the model to recognize the “Full” <math id="S2.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> “House” dependency. This mutual coordination before discretization prevents the error and leads to the correct response. The Ġ symbol (<em id="S2.F2.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S2.F2.6.2" class="ltx_text"></span> in ĠFlush indicates a token that begins with a whitespace.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Inference Acceleration Technique of DLLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The acceleration of inference for DLLMs is an area of active research, as reducing latency and computational overhead is crucial for real-world applications. Broadly, two primary domains have emerged for accelerating inference: dynamic caching mechanism and optimized sampling strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic Caching Mechanism.</span> The caching mechanism for diffusion models differs from AR models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, primarily due to the bidirectional attention mechanism employed in diffusion processes.
Several approaches have optimized caching in this context:
Fast-dLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> accelerates this by using chunked KV caching and confidence-aware parallel decoding;
dKV-Cache <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> employs delayed caching to reuse key-value states after token decoding, ensuring stable acceleration;
and Sparse-dLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> reduces overhead by pruning less important tokens in the cache.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optimized Sampling Strategy.</span> Beyond dynamic caching, optimizing the sampling process itself represents a pivotal direction for accelerating inference in DLLMs.
Research has explored diverse strategies:
WINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> introduces revocable decoding to enhance both efficiency and effectiveness in DLLMs;
similarly, FreeDave <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> enables lossless parallel decoding via self-drafting and self-verification;
COVER <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> performs context-preserving verification via KV-cache override to reduce flip-flop revisions in revocable diffusion decoding.
and Learn2PD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> trains a lightweight filter model to predict token-wise convergence, reducing redundant decoding.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminary</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decoding Process of DLLMs.</span> Let <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be the vocabulary, in which <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{[MASK]}\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>[MASK]</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{[MASK]}\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is a special token. Given a prompt <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, a DLLM parameterized with <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is designed to generate a response <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=[y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{L}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=[y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{L}]</annotation></semantics></math> with a predefined response length <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. The response sequence is initialized as all mask tokens, <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\leftarrow[\text{[MASK]},\text{[MASK]},\ldots,\text{[MASK]}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mtext>[MASK]</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>[MASK]</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>[MASK]</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\leftarrow[\text{[MASK]},\text{[MASK]},\ldots,\text{[MASK]}]</annotation></semantics></math>. The decoding process iteratively refines the response sequence <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>
in each decoding step. We model the decoding process as a state transition, where each masked token (state <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>) is transformed into a specific semantic token (state <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>) under certain rules.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, at each decoding step, the model estimates the probability distribution over the response sequence as <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\hat{Y}|X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\hat{Y}|X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Tokens that meet the <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐓</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> transition condition are selected to be unmasked.</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}\leftarrow{\arg\max}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}p_{\theta}(\hat{y_{i}}=v|X,Y),\ \text{if}\ i\in\mathrm{rule}_{\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{T}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>rule</mi><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐓</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\leftarrow{\arg\max}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}p_{\theta}(\hat{y_{i}}=v|X,Y),\ \text{if}\ i\in\mathrm{rule}_{\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{T}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{rule}_{\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>rule</mi><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐓</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{rule}_{\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of positions that satisfy the <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐓</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> transition condition.
For example, under the commonly used high-confidence greedy decoding, we have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{rule}_{\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{T}}=\{\mathop{\arg\max}_{i\in\{i|y_{i}=\text{[MASK]}\}}(\max_{v\in\mathcal{V}}p_{\theta}(\hat{y_{i}}=v|X,Y)\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>rule</mi><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐓</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><munder><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>[MASK]</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{rule}_{\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{T}}=\{\mathop{\arg\max}_{i\in\{i|y_{i}=\text{[MASK]}\}}(\max_{v\in\mathcal{V}}p_{\theta}(\hat{y_{i}}=v|X,Y)\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The decoding process concludes once every element in <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> has transitioned to state <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> as the final generated result.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Motivation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Current DLLMs still face the speed-performance trade-off, with optimal performance typically achieved when decoding one token at a time. Parallel decoding, while faster, often results in a performance drop due to “combinatorial contradiction,” where sampling multiple tokens from different plausible completion paths leads to mutual contradictions. We attribute this issue to the strictly <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">discrete</em> nature of parallel decoding, where simultaneous sampling of multiple positions lacks coordination, causing semantic conflicts. To address this, we propose introducing a continuous intermediate state between the two discrete states, state <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and state <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, allowing positions to accumulate and align decoding intent before finalizing the output, thereby resolving inconsistencies and improving performance (see <a href="#S2.F2" title="In 2.1 Diffusion-based Large Language Model ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Rejection Mixing: Fast Semantic Propagation of Mask Tokensfor Efficient DLLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Rejection Mixing of Continuous States</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on the motivation above, we propose Rejection Mixing (ReMix), a method designed to accelerate semantic propagation in DLLM inference by introducing a continuous intermediate state <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math> into the standard discrete <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐓</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> transition. This state allows for mutual perception and iterative adjustment of inter-positional inconsistencies before collapsing into a final discrete state T.
Operationally, each position starts as a masked token in state <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. At each decoding step, if the decoding rule is satisfied, the position is directly decoded to the token state <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, it shifts from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to the continuous state <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, where it is iteratively updated by the mixing rule, and either be accepted to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> once the decoding rule holds or rejected back from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> according to the rejection rule (detailed in  <a href="#alg1" title="In 4.2 Rejection Mixing of Continuous States ‣ 4 Method ‣ Rejection Mixing: Fast Semantic Propagation of Mask Tokensfor Efficient DLLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
We now detail the specific rules governing the transitions of each position among states <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialization (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span> As introduced in <a href="#S3" title="3 Preliminary ‣ Rejection Mixing: Fast Semantic Propagation of Mask Tokensfor Efficient DLLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the decoding process begins with the entire response sequence initialized with mask tokens <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> at every position.</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}\leftarrow\text{[MASK]},\ \forall i\in\{1,\ldots,L\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mtext>[MASK]</mtext></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\leftarrow\text{[MASK]},\ \forall i\in\{1,\ldots,L\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(Initialization)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decoding Rule (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{M},\mathbf{C})\rightarrow\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐓</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{M},\mathbf{C})\rightarrow\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span> For adaptive parallel decoding, we employ a commonly used confidence-based thresholding strategy. Specifically, when the confidence of the output distribution at a given position surpasses a predefined threshold <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math>, that position is decoded into a concrete semantic token, corresponding to a transition from state <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math>) to state <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.Ex4X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{i}\leftarrow{\arg\max}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}\,p_{\theta}(\hat{y_{i}}=v|X,Y)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{i}\leftarrow{\arg\max}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}\,p_{\theta}(\hat{y_{i}}=v|X,Y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(<math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{M},\mathbf{C})\rightarrow\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐓</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{M},\mathbf{C})\rightarrow\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.Ex4Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{if}\ {\max}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}p_{\theta}(\hat{y_{i}}=v|X,Y)&gt;\tau_{\text{conf}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mo lspace="0.667em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{if}\ {\max}_{v\in\mathcal{V}}p_{\theta}(\hat{y_{i}}=v|X,Y)&gt;\tau_{\text{conf}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mixing Rule (<math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{C}\circlearrowright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐂</mi></mrow><mo>↻</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{C}\circlearrowright</annotation></semantics></math>).</span> The Mixing Rule defines the transition from state <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to the continuous state <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as the self-transition process within <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous state rather than a discrete token, the transition to <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented as an update to the embedding <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y}^{\text{emb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mtext>emb</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y}^{\text{emb}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For discrete tokens, let the embedding layer be parameterized by a weight matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|</annotation></semantics></math> is the vocabulary size and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding dimension. Because the mapping between a token and its embedding is one-to-one, updating the token <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> can be equivalently viewed as updating its embedding <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\text{emb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mtext>emb</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\text{emb}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our Mixing Rule is designed to preserve the output distribution information from the previous step to enhance consistency in subsequent decoding.</p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.Ex5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{i}^{\text{emb}}\leftarrow\beta W^{\top}p_{\theta}(\hat{y_{i}}|X,Y)+(1-\beta)\mathrm{Emb}_{\text{[MASK]}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>emb</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Emb</mi><mtext>[MASK]</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{i}^{\text{emb}}\leftarrow\beta W^{\top}p_{\theta}(\hat{y_{i}}|X,Y)+(1-\beta)\mathrm{Emb}_{\text{[MASK]}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(<math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{C}\circlearrowright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐂</mi></mrow><mo>↻</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}\rightarrow\mathbf{C}\circlearrowright</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.Ex5Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{if position $i$ not decoded}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>if position </mtext><mi>i</mi><mtext> not decoded</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{if position $i$ not decoded}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Emb}_{\text{[MASK]}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Emb</mi><mtext>[MASK]</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Emb}_{\text{[MASK]}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the embedding corresponding to the [MASK] token, and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the mixing coefficient between the output distribution and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Emb}_{\text{[MASK]}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Emb</mi><mtext>[MASK]</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Emb}_{\text{[MASK]}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, when converting the output distribution into an embedding update (<em id="S4.SS2.p4.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, computing <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\top}p_{\theta}(\hat{y}_{i}|X,Y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\top}p_{\theta}(\hat{y}_{i}|X,Y))</annotation></semantics></math>, we employ an adaptive top-<math id="S4.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> sampling strategy that adjusts the threshold by confidence and consolidates residual mass into [MASK] for stability (see full details in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Adaptive Top-𝑝 Sampling in Mixing Rule ‣ Rejection Mixing: Fast Semantic Propagation of Mask Tokensfor Efficient DLLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> ReMix</figcaption>
